---
import Document from '../layouts/Document.astro';
import { localUrl } from '../data/site';
const sections = [{id:'status',title:'Current availability'},{id:'connection',title:'The intended connection'},{id:'uploads',title:'Uploads & permissions'},{id:'disconnect',title:'Disconnecting'},{id:'data',title:'Where data goes'}];
---
<Document title="Your account. Your assets" description="Account linking is designed to remove the image-upload detour while keeping you in control of your Roblox authorization." eyebrow="ACCOUNT LINKING" sections={sections}>
  <section id="status"><div class="notice"><strong>Hosted linking is not live</strong><p>The account service is prepared in the project, but it has not been deployed or tested with real public authorization and uploads. Domain registration does not make it available. Continue with manual image uploads and asset-ID mapping.</p></div><h2>Keep building with manual mappings</h2><p>Download the collected PNGs from FrameForge’s Assets tab, upload them through Roblox, and map the returned asset IDs. Editable UI conversion works without a hosted account service.</p><p><a class="button secondary" href={localUrl('guide/#image-mapping')}>Follow the image-mapping guide</a></p></section>
  <section id="connection"><h2>A connection that starts in Figma</h2><p>Once the separately hosted service is deployed and verified, the intended flow is:</p><figure class="guide-diagram"><b>Connect Roblox in Figma</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Approve access in Roblox</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Return to Figma</b><figcaption>FrameForge checks the pending connection automatically inside the plugin.</figcaption></figure><p>You authorize your own Roblox account. The connection must stay bound to the Figma plugin session that requested it, so this website does not have a login button or an independent OAuth shortcut.</p><p>The normal user will not need to run a helper, obtain an API key, copy a pairing code, or manually check connection status. Those tools belong to advanced development workflows.</p></section>
  <section id="uploads"><h2>Choose when images are uploaded</h2><ul><li>The implemented hosted flow targets the linked <strong>personal Roblox account</strong>. It does not promise group uploading.</li><li>Automatic upload is a user-controlled option.</li><li>Already mapped images are skipped; missing images receive their asset IDs after a successful upload.</li><li>Roblox may still process or moderate images and restrict which experiences can use them.</li></ul><p>Uploading an asset and having permission to display it are separate steps. Test image access in the intended experience.</p></section>
  <section id="disconnect"><h2>Disconnect when you choose</h2><p>The plugin clears its saved connection credential when you disconnect. The service invalidates the connection and attempts to revoke the Roblox refresh token. If the service cannot be reached, follow the plugin’s message and revoke the authorization through Roblox too.</p><p>Disconnecting does not delete assets already uploaded to Roblox. Upload-operation records and image-to-asset mappings can also outlast the connection under the current service implementation. Their retention and deletion process must be confirmed before public launch.</p></section>
  <section id="data"><h2>A separate, hosted service</h2><p>Native UI conversion runs in Figma. When hosted upload is enabled in a future release, requested image bytes pass through an HTTPS service to Roblox. Roblox authorization credentials stay encrypted on that service; the plugin stores an opaque, revocable connection credential in private Figma storage.</p><p>The public website does not handle your OAuth session. GitHub Pages serves the documentation; it cannot run the Node OAuth/upload service. <code>api.frameforgeui.website</code> is a proposed backend address, not a verified live endpoint.</p><p><a href={localUrl('privacy/')}>Read the privacy review draft</a> for the inspected behavior and decisions still needed before launch.</p></section>
</Document>
